lesson:
An **interface** is a contract: a list of methods a class promises to have, without saying how they work.

```
interface Damageable {
    void takeDamage(int amount);
    int getHealth();
}
```

Any class can sign it with `implements Damageable`, and the compiler makes sure it really has those methods. A player, a wooden crate and a stone wall are very different things, but all three can take damage:

```
class Crate implements Damageable {
    private int health = 30;
    public void takeDamage(int amount) { health = Math.max(0, health - amount); }
    public int getHealth() { return health; }
}
```

**Why bother?** Because the rest of your program can now work with "anything damageable" without knowing what it is:

```
static void explode(List<Damageable> nearby) {
    for (Damageable d : nearby) d.takeDamage(40);
}
```

This is **programming to the interface**. When you add a new kind of thing next month (a barrel, a door), `explode` doesn't change at all. You write the new class, it implements the interface, done. Large programs stay manageable this way.

A few rules:
- Interface methods are public and (normally) have no body. The implementing class must mark them `public`.
- A class can implement **several** interfaces: `class Barrel implements Damageable, Burnable`.
- You can't do `new Damageable()`; you make objects of the classes, but variables and lists can have the interface type: `Damageable d = new Crate();`.
- A **default method** has a body that every implementer gets for free, built from the other methods: `default boolean isDestroyed() { return getHealth() <= 0; }`. A class may still override it.
- An interface with exactly one abstract method is a **functional interface**, so you can pass a lambda: `Notifier n = (to, msg) -> System.out.println(to + ": " + msg);`.

A very practical use: a clinic reminder program needs to send messages. Define `interface Notifier { void send(String to, String message); }`. The real program uses an SMS class; your tests use a `MemoryNotifier` that just stores messages in a list so you can check them. Same code, swapped part. Robots do this with sensors and motors: code talks to `DistanceSensor`, and you can plug in a real one or a fake one.

The common mistake is making interfaces too big. Keep them small and about one ability ("can take damage", "can send a message").

example.code:
import java.util.List;

interface Sensor {
    double read();
    String unit();
    default String report() { return read() + " " + unit(); }
}

class TankLevel implements Sensor {
    public double read() { return 320.5; }
    public String unit() { return "litres"; }
}

class SoilMoisture implements Sensor {
    public double read() { return 41.0; }
    public String unit() { return "%"; }
    @Override public String report() { return "soil " + read() + unit(); }
}

public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Sensor> sensors = List.of(new TankLevel(), new SoilMoisture());
        for (Sensor s : sensors) System.out.println(s.report());

        Sensor fake = new Sensor() {          // a quick test double
            public double read() { return 0; }
            public String unit() { return "test"; }
        };
        System.out.println(fake.report());
    }
}

